<stdio.h> (Standard Input/Output):
Provides functions for input and output operations, including printf, scanf, fopen, fclose, and more.
Used for reading and writing to files, as well as for console input and output.

<ctype.h> (Character Handling):
Contains functions for character classification and conversion, such as isalpha, isdigit, tolower, and toupper.
  
<stdbool.h> (Boolean Types):
Defines the bool type and boolean constants true and false.
Used for boolean operations and conditional expressions.
  
<stddef.h> (Standard Definitions):
Defines several standard macros, such as NULL, size_t, and ptrdiff_t.
Used for working with pointers, memory allocation, and array indexing.

<stdint.h> (Standard Integer Types):
Defines fixed-size integer types like int8_t, int16_t, int32_t, uint8_t, and uint16_t, which guarantee a specific number of bits.
Useful for writing portable code that relies on specific integer sizes.
  
<time.h> (Date and Time):
Provides functions and types for working with date and time values.
Used for tasks involving date and time calculations.

<limits.h> (Implementation-Defined Constants):
Defines implementation-specific constants, such as the minimum and maximum values for integer types.
Helps in writing code that depends on the characteristics of the underlying system.
  
<errno.h> (Error Codes):
Defines error codes and provides access to the global errno variable.
Used for error handling and reporting.
  
<assert.h> (Assertions):
Provides the assert macro for inserting debugging assertions into your code.
Useful for verifying conditions during debugging.

<stdarg.h> (Variable Argument Lists):
Contains functions and macros for working with variable-length argument lists, such as those used in printf and scanf functions.
Used when defining functions that accept a variable number of arguments.
